\fs32 \cf4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The 
\fs28 BigInt
\fs32  implementation uses a base-2^32 representation with a little-endian format, where the least significant word is stored at index 0. Each word is a 
\fs28 UInt32
\fs32 , allowing for efficient storage and arithmetic operations on large integers. This design choice optimizes performance for binary computations while still supporting arbitrary precision. 
\f2 \cf7 \ul \uc0\u8617 
\f0 \cf4 \ulnone \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Built on top of our completed BigInt10 implementation, BigDecimal will support arbitrary precision for both the integer and fractional parts, similar to 
\fs28 decimal
\fs32  and 
\fs28 mpmath
\fs32  in Python, 
\fs28 java.math.BigDecimal
\fs32  in Java, etc. 
\f2 \cf7 \ul \uc0\u8617 
\f0 \cf4 \ulnone \
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The 
\fs28 Decimal128
\fs32  type can represent values with up to 29 significant digits and a maximum of 28 digits after the decimal point. When a value exceeds the maximum representable value (
\fs28 2^96 - 1
\fs32 ), Decimo either raises an error or rounds the value to fit within these constraints. For example, the significant digits of 
\fs28 8.8888888888888888888888888888
\fs32  (29 eights total with 28 after the decimal point) exceeds the maximum representable value (
\fs28 2^96 - 1
\fs32 ) and is automatically rounded to 
\fs28 8.888888888888888888888888889
\fs32  (28 eights total with 27 after the decimal point). Decimo's 
\fs28 Decimal128
\fs32  type is similar to 
\fs28 System.Decimal
\fs32  (C#/.NET), 
\fs28 rust_decimal
\fs32  in Rust, 
\fs28 DECIMAL/NUMERIC
\fs32  in SQL Server, etc. 
\f2 \cf7 \ul \uc0\u8617 
\f0 \cf4 \ulnone \
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The BigInt10 implementation uses a base-10 representation for users (maintaining decimal semantics), while internally using an optimized base-10^9 storage system for efficient calculations. This approach balances human-readable decimal operations with high-performance computing. It provides both floor division (round toward negative infinity) and truncate division (round toward zero) semantics, enabling precise handling of division operations with correct mathematical behavior regardless of operand signs. 
\f2 \cf7 \ul \uc0\u8617 
\f0 \cf4 \ulnone \
